        {
          "say": "Gradient is rise over run. 6 over 4 is three over two.",
          "write": "m = 6 ÷ 4 = 3/2"
        },
        {
          "say": "So the line goes up 1.5 for every 1 across.",
          "write": "up 1.5 for every 1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "The gradient formula",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "For any two points, subtract the y's on top and the x's on the bottom.",
          "write": "m = (y₂ − y₁) ÷ (x₂ − x₁)"
        },
        {
          "say": "Try (−3, 4) and (3, 0). The rise is 0 minus 4, which is minus 4.",
          "write": "rise = 0 − 4 = −4",
          "diagram": "diagrams/y9/maths/gradient-neg.svg",
          "alt": "Line through (−3, 4) and (3, 0) with run 6 and rise −4"
        },
        {
          "say": "The run is 3 minus minus 3, which is 6.",
          "write": "run = 3 − (−3) = 6"
        },
        {
          "say": "Minus 4 over 6 simplifies to minus two thirds.",
          "write": "m = −4 ÷ 6 = −⅔"
        },
        {
          "say": "The line goes down, so a negative gradient makes sense.",
          "write": "downhill → negative ✓"
        }
      ]
    },
